.TH MOS 1 "2026-06-20" "mac-optical-state" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mos \- report the state of a macOS optical drive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mos
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ drive ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B mos
.I drive
.RI "(bare selector runs " state )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mos
reports what a macOS optical drive is actually doing \(em tray open, tray
closed on an empty slot, loading, unit ready, busy \(em by querying the drive
directly over MMC. Unlike
.BR drutil (1),
which prints
.I No Media Inserted
for both an open tray and a closed-but-empty one,
.B mos
distinguishes them.
.PP
A state query costs at most two MMC commands (TEST UNIT READY plus a tray
probe or profile read). It needs no entitlements and no root \(em only the
console user's SCSITaskUserClient grant on the drive.
.PP
.B mos
is also an embeddable pure-C library; see
.B mos.h
and the project documentation.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED SUBCOMMANDS — gen-cli-docs.py (edit cli/<verb>.c descriptors)
.TP
.BR state " [drive]"
Report drive state (default verb)
.TP
.B list
List all drives with their states
.TP
.BR watch " [drive]"
Stream state events as NDJSON until SIGINT
.TP
.BR metadata " [drive]"
Disc identity record (profile, TOC, volume)
.TP
.BR drive " [drive]"
Drive facts (identity, protection, firmware)
.TP
.BR features " [drive]"
MMC feature list with current bits
.TP
.BR tray " <action> [drive]"
Control the tray (eject, close, lock, unlock)
.TP
.BR capacity " [drive]"
Disc capacity (media size + free/append space)
.TP
.BR probe " <drive>"
Diagnostic notification stream (mos.probe.v0)
.\" END GENERATED SUBCOMMANDS
.PP
Each subcommand's options, the drive selector, and exit codes are detailed
in the sections below.
.SH DRIVE SELECTOR
The
.I drive
is a positional argument, like
.BR "diskutil info disk4" .
With a single drive attached it may be omitted; with several it is
required. Three forms:
.TP
.B Index
A 1-based index from the
.B Index
column of
.BR "mos list" ;
all digits (for example,
.BR 1 ).
Positional and not durable \(em it races device hot-plug. Use it only for
one-shot, human-driven invocations.
.TP
.B registry_id
The drive's attachment identity, as printed in the
.B Registry ID:
row or the JSON
.B registry_id
field; all digits, at or above 2^32. Never reused; stable for the life of
an attachment.
.TP
.B BSD form
.BR disk4 ", " rdisk4 ", or " /dev/disk4 .
.PP
.RB ( probe
accepts an index or BSD form only, no registry_id.)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-index " \fIN\fR"
1-based drive index; the explicit form of the positional Index selector.
.TP
.BR \-\-bsd " \fINAME\fR"
BSD form; the explicit form of the positional BSD selector.
.TP
.BR \-\-registry " \fIID\fR"
registry_id (the durable selector for scripts); the explicit form of the
positional registry_id selector.
.TP
.B \-\-force
With
.BR "tray eject" ,
additionally clear a
.B COLD
tray Prevent
.B LOCK
in the way \(em a deliberately\-locked idle drive, no mount in play \(em then
eject. It does
.B not
force the filesystem. A plain
.B "tray eject"
already unmounts a mounted disc
.BR gracefully ,
exactly like
.BR "diskutil unmountDisk diskN" ,
clears the removal lock macOS arms on mount, and
.B never
kills open file handles \(em a busy volume is reported as
.B busy
(close the open files, or unmount with
.B diskutil
first). The only thing
.B \-\-force
adds is clearing a cold Prevent lock; the one blocker it cannot clear is another
program holding the drive exclusively, reported as
.BR exclusive_access .
.TP
.B \-\-dump
With
.BR probe :
one-shot DiscRecording dictionary capture (text + XML plists; takes no
drive argument).
.TP
.B \-\-capture
With
.BR "probe " <drive> :
issue the fixed menu of known MMC commands and emit each raw reply as
NDJSON
.RI ( mos.capture.v0 )
for fixture capture. Read-only; capture an unmounted disc.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Emit JSON
.RI ( mos.state.v1 " / " mos.error.v1 " / " mos.list.v1 ).
Takes no argument \(em each schema name carries its own version.
.TP
.BR \-\-pairs
Flatten the JSON document to dotted
.IR key = value
lines (one scalar per line, e.g.\&
.IR speeds.max_read_kbps =35980;
nested objects join with a dot and arrays index with
.RB \(lq [i] \(rq ),
for
.BR grep / awk .
Implies
.BR \-\-json .
One-shot verbs only \(em not the streaming
.B watch
or
.BR probe .
.TP
.BR \-\-color " \fIWHEN\fR"
Colorize human output:
.B auto
(the default \(em color when stdout is a terminal and neither
.B NO_COLOR
nor
.B "TERM=dumb"
is set),
.B always
(force color, e.g. when piping to
.BR "less \-R" ),
or
.BR never .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable color; an alias for
.BR "\-\-color never" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit. With a subcommand
.RI ( "mos " <verb> " \-h" ),
show that verb's help and examples.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The following variables apply to
.B watch
only. Each is an integer number of milliseconds in the range
0..3600000, where 0 selects the library default; an out-of-range or
non-numeric value warns on stderr and falls back to the default.
.TP
.B MOS_WATCH_STABLE_MS
Poll period in stable states (default 2000).
.TP
.B MOS_WATCH_TRANSITION_MS
Poll period in transitional states (default 200).
.SH STATES
.BR open ", " empty ", " loading ", " ready ", " busy ", " formatting ", "
.BR media_unreadable ", " device_fault ", " empty_or_open ", " unknown .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B mos
uses
.BR sysexits.h (3)
codes:
.TP
.B 0
A drive state was observed (including
.BR unknown ,
which is a valid result).
.TP
.B 64
Usage error
.RB ( EX_USAGE ).
.TP
.B 66
No such device
.RB ( EX_NOINPUT ).
.TP
.B 69
Unavailable \(em driver rejected or command unsupported
.RB ( EX_UNAVAILABLE ).
.TP
.B 70
Internal error
.RB ( EX_SOFTWARE ).
.TP
.B 71
OS error
.RB ( EX_OSERR ).
.TP
.B 74
I/O error
.RB ( EX_IOERR ).
.TP
.B 75
Temporary failure \(em busy, contended, or timed out
.RB ( EX_TEMPFAIL ).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Report the state of the only attached drive:
.B mos state
.TP
Report drive 1 as JSON:
.B mos state 1 \-\-json
.TP
List every drive:
.B mos list
.TP
Act on each disc that becomes readable, hot-plug included:
.B mos watch | jq ...
.TP
Eject a drive, clearing a tray lock in the way (graceful unmount, never forces the filesystem):
.B mos tray eject disk4 \-\-force
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR drutil (1),
.BR diskutil (8),
.BR sysexits (3)
.PP
